Align the echo controller again at an unmute after a change of devices, forgetting only whether there was echo

Patch 0011 makes the echo controller anew at the first start of recording (an unmute)
after any change of audio devices, so that a headset taken off for speakers while the
microphone was off does not keep what AEC3 learned of the headset: that there is no
echo at all, its transparent mode, which let the speakers' echo through (patch 0010
alone: 0.9 s at -14 dBFS; with 0011, 0.3 s at -25 dBFS). Since patch 0014 a change while
recording runs is followed by aligning the echo controller again at once, and a new
controller at the next unmute starts from nothing in the middle of the other side's
speech. Measured in the Linux call lab (tools/call-lab/device-echo.sh, the other side
talking, each output looped back into the microphone; 6 unmutes per case and rule,
interleaved): a controller made anew at the unmute let the other side's speech back for
1.6 to 1.9 s (bursts of 3 to 4.8 s) in every case with speakers, the output or the
microphone changed while unmuted or while muted and 0011's own case of a headset taken
off for speakers while muted; and after speakers taken off for a headset it held the
words said on the headset down (7.7 dB at the median, 64 % of the syllables' starts
more than 6 dB down). Aligned again keeping everything, those came to 0.2 to 0.4 s and
2 dB, but in 0011's case measured as 0011 was (mic.sh unmute with UNMUTE_UNPLUG: the
headset used for half a minute, the output taken away while muted) the transparent mode
kept let the speakers' echo through for 0.25 to 0.45 s at -10 dBFS (a new controller:
0.05 to 0.15 s at -12 to -20 dBFS).

At the first start of recording after a change of devices that no restart while
recording handled, the audio state now has the echo controller aligned again forgetting
only its transparent mode (AudioProcessing::RestartEchoControllerForOtherDevices,
EchoControl::RestartForOtherDevices; in AEC3, patch 0010's restart for recording without
keeping the transparent mode: both filters and the ERLE start over, the suppressor's and
the residual echo's models stay), in place of 0011's new controller. A change while
recording runs is handled at the device's own restart by patch 0014's realign, which
keeps the transparent mode, and is noted as handled (AudioDevicesHandledCount,
api/audio/audio_device.h), so the next unmute, maybe minutes later on the same devices,
does not take it again. A call's start still makes the echo controller anew. With the
echo canceller off nothing is made.

Measured as above, at the unmute: 0011's case 0.05 to 0.1 s over -35 dBFS, -24 to -26
dBFS at the loudest (the first second at -38 dBFS, against -25 with a new controller and
-20 aligned again keeping everything); a headset taken off for speakers while muted 0.18
s over the floor at -53 dBFS; the output changed while muted 0.38 s at -34 dBFS, bursts
of 0.2 to 0.4 s; the microphone changed while muted 0.23 s; speakers taken off for a
headset while muted, the words on the headset 1.9 dB down. The output or the microphone
changed while unmuted, then an unmute (the change handled at the restart): 0.33 and 0.23
s (a new controller: 1.85 and 1.78 s; aligned again: 0.23 and 0.25 s). Forgetting the
transparent mode at the device's restart while recording too was tried and kept out: in
restart-echo.sh's rig (12 output changes per cell, interleaved with patch 0014 alone) it
let bursts of 0.2 to 0.4 s through in 3 of 24 changes with speakers (0014 alone: 1 of
24) and held a headset's first words down (14 % of the syllables' starts more than 6 dB
down, none with 0014 alone); with this patch the restart is 0014's (8 changes per cell:
1 burst of 0.2 s in 16 with speakers, a headset's words untouched).

A headset taken off for speakers while unmuted: the change is handled at the device's
restart, which keeps the transparent mode the headset gave, and nothing forgets it
afterwards. Measured (device-echo.sh's c3: 14 s on a headset, then the speakers chosen
in the call, 12 changes each, interleaved with patch 0014 and this patch left out): the
other side's speech came back for 0.1 s [0, 0.65] over the floor, -59 dBFS at the
loudest at the median, faint bursts (0.2 to 0.45 s) in 5 of 12, the last of it above
-70 dBFS 1.75 s [0, 5] after the change; without these patches 0.35 s [0.2, 0.85] at
-18 dBFS, bursts in 11 of 12 (up to 0.85 s), over by 0.75 s [0.5, 8.6]. a's words said
right after the change, in double talk: 1.4 dB down at the median (0.6 without).

Not measured: a real headset and speakers.
